= Anchoring effect — Simple =

The '''anchoring effect''' happens when an early number or starting point pulls a later estimate toward it.

If two people are asked the same question after seeing different starting numbers, their answers can end up closer to those different numbers.

Anchors can matter even when they are not very useful.

Scientists do not think every anchoring effect has one single cause. Sometimes people adjust too little from a starting point; in other cases the anchor changes which information becomes easy to think about.

